BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Jacobs School of Music
DTSTART:20260710T230000Z
DTEND:20260711T010000Z
LOCATION:Recital Hall
SUMMARY:William Harvey\, violin\; Malcolm Liu\, piano
DESCRIPTION:Repertoire  Messiaen: Thème et variations (1932)Fazil Say: S
 onata\, Op. 7 (1997)Sauret: 'A Péra' from "Souvenir d'Orient\, Op. 63" (
 1901)Gity Razaz: Creation of the Birds (2023)Cowell: Selections from "Hom
 age to Iran" (1957)Reena Esmail: When the Violin (2018/20)Rimsky-Korsakov
 /Powell: 'Song of the Indian Guest (Song of India)' from "Sadko" (1898)Ru
 o Huang: Four Fragments (2006)Takemitsu: Distance de fée (1951)Kreisler:
  Tambourin chinois\, Op. 3 (1910)About the Artists  Concertmaster of Mexi
 co's premier orchestra\, the Orquesta Sinfónica Nacional\, and violin fa
 culty at the Universidad Panamericana\, violinist\, conductor\, and compo
 ser William Harvey has appeared as violin soloist at Carnegie Hall with t
 he New York Youth Symphony and has performed concerti with orchestras in 
 Argentina\, El Salvador\, the Philippines\, Mexico\, and the United State
 s. His recording of the violin concerto by Hector Infanzón garnered two 
 nominations to the Latin Grammys in 2021. For four years\, he served as t
 he violin and viola teacher at Afghanistan National Institute of Music (A
 NIM)\, founding the Afghan Youth Orchestra (AYO)\, which he conducted for
  President Hamid Karzai and led on a historic tour of the United States i
 n concerts at Carnegie Hall and the Kennedy Center. Harvey also conducted
  AYO on the 2012 and 2013 season finales of Afghan Star. He also publishe
 d anthologies of Afghan songs arranged for strings. He has previously ser
 ved as concertmaster of the Orquesta Sinfónica Sinaloa Las Artes (Mexico
 )\, Orquesta Sinfónica de la Universidad Nacional de San Juan (Argentina
 ) and the Spokane Symphony\, faculty at Indiana University's Summer Strin
 g Academy\, fellow at Carnegie Hall's Academy\, and guest artist at the M
 yanmar Music Festival\, Mehli Mehta Music Foundation\, and Iguazu en Conc
 ierto. He has performed at festivals throughout Mexico\, including the Fe
 stival del Centro Histórico. As a conductor\, he has led professional or
 chestras throughout Mexico and youth orchestras in Argentina\, Qatar\, th
 e Philippines\, Zimbabwe\, and the United States. Harvey's compositions h
 ave received over a hundred performances. Harvey earned his M.M. from The
  Juilliard School\, where he studied violin with Ronald Copes and composi
 tion with Samuel Adler\, and B.M. from the IU Jacobs School of Music\, wh
 ere he studied violin with Mimi Zweig and composition with Sven-David San
 dström. In 2005\, Harvey founded Cultures in Harmony. CiH workshops in P
 akistan\, Qatar\, Egypt\, the Philippines\, Tunisia\, Zimbabwe\, and Mexi
 co have benefited thousands of young musicians. Harvey plays on violins m
 ade by Joseph Curtin (2001) and Nadia Sánchez (2021).Biography for Malco
 lm Liu forthcoming
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Repertoire</strong><br />  Messia
 en: <em>Thème et variations (1932)<br /></em>Fazil Say: <em>Sonata\, Op.
  7 (1997)<br /></em>Sauret: <em>'A Péra' from "Souvenir d'Orient\, Op. 6
 3" (1901)<br /></em>Gity Razaz: <em>Creation of the Birds (2023)<br /></e
 m>Cowell: <em>Selections from "Homage to Iran" (1957)<br /></em>Reena Esm
 ail: <em>When the Violin (2018/20)<br /></em>Rimsky-Korsakov/Powell: <em>
 'Song of the Indian Guest (Song of India)' from "Sadko" (1898)<br /></em>
 Ruo Huang: <em>Four Fragments (2006)<br /></em>Takemitsu: <em>Distance de
  fée (1951)<br /></em>Kreisler: <em>Tambourin chinois\, Op. 3 (1910)<br 
 /></em><br /><strong>About the Artists</strong><br /><br />  Concertmaste
 r of Mexico's premier orchestra\, the Orquesta Sinfónica Nacional\, and 
 violin faculty at the Universidad Panamericana\, violinist\, conductor\, 
 and composer <strong>William Harvey</strong> has appeared as violin soloi
 st at Carnegie Hall with the New York Youth Symphony and has performed co
 ncerti with orchestras in Argentina\, El Salvador\, the Philippines\, Mex
 ico\, and the United States. His recording of the violin concerto by Hect
 or Infanzón garnered two nominations to the Latin Grammys in 2021. For f
 our years\, he served as the violin and viola teacher at Afghanistan Nati
 onal Institute of Music (ANIM)\, founding the Afghan Youth Orchestra (AYO
 )\, which he conducted for President Hamid Karzai and led on a historic t
 our of the United States in concerts at Carnegie Hall and the Kennedy Cen
 ter. Harvey also conducted AYO on the 2012 and 2013 season finales of <em
 >Afghan Star</em>. He also published anthologies of Afghan songs arranged
  for strings. He has previously served as concertmaster of the Orquesta S
 infónica Sinaloa Las Artes (Mexico)\, Orquesta Sinfónica de la Universi
 dad Nacional de San Juan (Argentina) and the Spokane Symphony\, faculty a
 t Indiana University's Summer String Academy\, fellow at Carnegie Hall's 
 Academy\, and guest artist at the Myanmar Music Festival\, Mehli Mehta Mu
 sic Foundation\, and Iguazu en Concierto. He has performed at festivals t
 hroughout Mexico\, including the Festival del Centro Histórico. As a con
 ductor\, he has led professional orchestras throughout Mexico and youth o
 rchestras in Argentina\, Qatar\, the Philippines\, Zimbabwe\, and the Uni
 ted States. Harvey's compositions have received over a hundred performanc
 es. Harvey earned his M.M. from The Juilliard School\, where he studied v
 iolin with Ronald Copes and composition with Samuel Adler\, and B.M. from
  the IU Jacobs School of Music\, where he studied violin with Mimi Zweig 
 and composition with Sven-David Sandström. In 2005\, Harvey founded Cult
 ures in Harmony. CiH workshops in Pakistan\, Qatar\, Egypt\, the Philippi
 nes\, Tunisia\, Zimbabwe\, and Mexico have benefited thousands of young m
 usicians. Harvey plays on violins made by Joseph Curtin (2001) and Nadia 
 Sánchez (2021).<br /><br /><em>Biography for Malcolm Liu forthcoming</em
 ></p>
